72728-Freqish","type":"artist","urls":[]}},"/api/discogs/label/2311/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"2311","image":"https://i.discogs.com/58MHEZ0kBRytN0Ivvqvnirp57RFfiqWh1YGJLsn62ec/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTIzMTEt/MTUxNzAxMTEyMy00/NjEwLmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/-LMetdbnowPyQe0cQg_sghOOpMFYViLy8tO6GW_KiwM/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTIzMTEt/MTUxNzAxMTEyMy00/NjEwLmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"US label\r\nInterscope Records was founded by film producer [a=Ted Field] (through [l=Interscope Communications, Inc.]) and record producer [a=Jimmy Iovine] in 1989 as a small alternative label. In 1990, [l=Warner Music Group] purchased a 50% stake in Interscope Records to market and distribute their music products through [l=Atlantic] Records, a division of [l=Warner Music], which, at the time, was a part of [l=Time Warner].\r\n\r\nIn 1992, Marion \"Suge\" Knight's infamous \"gangsta rap\" label [l=Death Row Records (2)] joined the Interscope family. Death Row's first release was [a=Dr. Dre]'s [i]The Chronic[/i] album in December of 1992. November 1993 would see the biggest release for Interscope/Death Row, as [a=Snoop Dogg] released his debut album, [i]Doggystyle[/i], which went on to sell 3 million copies within several months of release. \r\n\r\nIn 1995, Warner Music Group (then known as [l=WEA International]) sold back its interest in Interscope Records. In 1996, [l=MCA, Inc.] (later to become [l=Universal Music Group], the world's biggest record company) purchased Interscope Records and would see most of its future releases distributed through them.\r\n\r\nIn August of 1997, Interscope had completely separated itself from the once highly successful [l=Death Row Records (2)], which had become independent briefly before signing a new distribution deal with [l=Koch Records] and ultimately going bankrupt. In 1998, [l=PolyGram] and [l=MCA] merged to create Universal Music Group. 1999 would see the merging of both [l=A&M Records] and [l=Geffen Records] into Interscope, creating the [b][l=Interscope Geffen A&M][/b] group. By 2004, Interscope had become one of UMG's most dominant labels, ousting its sister label [l=Def Jam Recordings].\r\n\r\nLabel Code: LC 06406 / LC 6406","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:2311","relations":{"parentLabel":{"id":1465258,"name":"Interscope Geffen A&M"},"sublabels":[{"id":3957408,"name":"#JUSTAREGULARDAY"},{"id":1091323,"name":"10 Summers Records"},{"id":695115,"name":"A Very Special Christmas"},{"id":2310,"name":"Aftermath Entertainment"},{"id":74898,"name":"Ambush Reality"},{"id":3331365,"name":"Bichota Records LLC"},{"id":1737995,"name":"Billion Dollar Baby"},{"id":3880899,"name":"Cole World, Inc."},{"id":4306702,"name":"Definitive Sound Series"},{"id":1860004,"name":"Dignity Records (3)"},{"id":1047624,"name":"DJ Snake Music"},{"id":1113780,"name":"Dreamville, Inc."},{"id":464281,"name":"EST19XX"},{"id":3938235,"name":"EST19XX, LLC"},{"id":34585,"name":"Flawless Records (2)"},{"id":754140,"name":"Friends Keep Secrets"},{"id":1385213,"name":"Interscope Latino Records"},{"id":354374,"name":"Interscope Records, Inc."},{"id":3473293,"name":"Interscope Vinyl Collective"},{"id":26524,"name":"JCOR Entertainment"},{"id":83162,"name":"Kickball Records"},{"id":755634,"name":"KonLive Distribution"},{"id":101139,"name":"Konvict Muzik"},{"id":3846527,"name":"Lil Monsters LLC"},{"id":752700,"name":"Mad Love Records (3)"},{"id":3628457,"name":"Music Soup"},{"id":52523,"name":"MySpace Records"},{"id":136969,"name":"N.E.E.T."},{"id":138432,"name":"Not On Label (Gwen Stefani)"},{"id":647,"name":"Nothing Records"},{"id":177398,"name":"Pussycat Dolls JV"},{"id":122077,"name":"Streamline Records"},{"id":846857,"name":"Studio 26, Miami, FL"},{"id":153369,"name":"Tennman Records"},{"id":102445,"name":"Tiny Evil"},{"id":47085,"name":"Trauma Records (2)"},{"id":1245393,"name":"We Are The World Foundation"}]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/2311-Interscope-Records","type":"label","urls":["https://www.interscope.com/","https://www.facebook.com/interscope","https://x.com/interscope","https://www.instagram.com/interscope/","https://www.youtube.com/interscope/","https://soundcloud.com/interscope","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interscope_Records"]}},"/api/discogs/label/2901398/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"2901398","image":"https://i.discogs.com/-yGACWjskh8fePJ_EJRF43j35gr_7ZN2Zs7IkpFaXrc/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:598/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTI5MDEz/OTgtMTY2MTkzNzgw/Mi02NDg2LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/1fuzsyRBSBYgW9sywBrKaMVNtvRwxbps-TrPqPHY758/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTI5MDEz/OTgtMTY2MTkzNzgw/Mi02NDg2LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"House label founded primarily to amplify the creative contributions of queer artists working in dance music.\r\nRun by [a=GIDEÖN].","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:2901398","relations":{"parentLabel":null,"sublabels":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/2901398-HOMO-CENTRIC-RECORDS","type":"label","urls":["https://www.instagram.com/homo.centric/","https://soundcloud.com/homo-centric-records","https://homo-centric-records.bandcamp.com/"]}},"/api/discogs/label/382/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"382","image":"https://i.discogs.com/WZSdaqCT8tGHWWqPMf2Uj0WwWsTwMAXctVl7Gm6f0wg/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:372/w:451/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTM4Mi0x/MjAwMTY0NDg0Lmpw/ZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/1JO8RuXLXqBUI9r01bGccF7EfUfcblbU7eSm9wSDLIo/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTM4Mi0x/MjAwMTY0NDg0Lmpw/ZWc.jpeg","profile":"The NRK record label was conceived in 1996 by founders [a=Nick Harris] and [a=Redg Weeks].\r\nSince 1992 NRK had been one of the UK's pioneering DJ Agencies, holding the books for a multitude of artists and DJs like Larry Heard, Derrick Carter, Carl Craig, Dimitri From Paris, Ian Pooley, Motorbass and Mousse T.\r\nThroughout their time as booking agents and promoters Nick & Redg yearned to channel their energy and enthusiasm directly into the music, they saw too many tired labels pidgeon-holing their output, terrified of eclecticism within the house sound.    \r\nSo, the NRK label was born, with King Street supremo Nelson Rosado penning the first release, \"House 'n' Kontrol\" back in 1996. The second release was to cement the reputation of Canada's Nick Holder as a leading light in deep house circles. \r\nHis friendship with Redg (half Canadian) led to the signing of \"Paradise\", a sun-kissed, balearic, deep-house classic, complemented by an outstanding mix from the Salt City Orchestra. The desirable NRK catalogue gained much momentum in its first year, with killer-cuts from Ian Pooley, The Plastic Avengers, Dimitri From Paris, Lenny Fontana and Freeform Five amongst others.\r\nThe label has now ceased trading.\r\n\r\nName variations as found on this profile:\r\n- NRK","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:382","relations":{"parentLabel":{"id":552458,"name":"NRK Sound Division Ltd."},"sublabels":[{"id":2123,"name":"Honchos Music"},{"id":4238357,"name":"The Lab (41)"}]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/382-NRK-Sound-Division","type":"label","urls":["http://nickharris1.bandcamp.com","http://nrkmusic.blogspot.com"]}},"/api/discogs/label/726365/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"726365","image":"https://i.discogs.com/buDSoQ7Mj9gYcxp-aiFhYJHTscxlWq4Bny9yWDKNSZo/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTcyNjM2/NS0xNzQ2MTIyMTEy/LTMyMjQuanBlZw.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/vhvO39u-foK30HwfzRRVnUDRrYHAPy93m4_GWxb8JoA/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTcyNjM2/NS0xNzQ2MTIyMTEy/LTMyMjQuanBlZw.jpeg","profile":"Electronic dance music label from London, UK\r\nStyle focus